# --- Virtual environments -------------------------------------------------
# The project's own environment is called venv-saas (not .venv), so it is
# named explicitly here. A virtual environment must never be committed: it is
# hundreds of megabytes, it is platform-specific, and it is fully reproducible
# from requirements.txt with a single command.
venv-saas/
.venv/
.venv-deploy/

# --- Python bytecode ------------------------------------------------------
# __pycache__ holds compiled versions of the .py files. They are regenerated
# automatically on import, so committing them adds noise and nothing else.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# --- Test output ----------------------------------------------------------
.pytest_cache/

# --- Deployment -----------------------------------------------------------
.vercel/

# --- Generated JSON: COMMITTED, and required for deployment ---------------
# reports/results.json is the ONLY data source the dashboard reads. The
# dashboard cannot compute it: it deliberately imports nothing heavier than
# pandas, and the deployment host caps a Python bundle at 500 MB against a ~1 GB
# analysis stack. So if results.json is not in the repository, the deployed
# dashboard has nothing to show. It is committed for that reason, not by
# accident.
#
# data_quality.json is committed alongside it for the same reason — the
# dashboard's Data Quality tab renders it.

# --- Generated data -------------------------------------------------------
# The data is produced by `python data_gen.py` and is reproducible from a
# single seed, so a stored copy proves nothing and adds 50 KB of churn to
# every commit. The generator IS the artefact; the CSV is a by-product.
data/raw/*.csv
data/raw/*.db
data/raw/*.zip

# --- Generated figures: COMMITTED, deliberately ----------------------------
# An earlier version of this file ignored these, reasoning that a committed
# image can drift out of step with the code that drew it. That is true, and it
# is outweighed here by something more important: this repository exists to be
# READ. The README refers to the figures by name, and a reference that points at
# nothing is worse than an image that might be one commit stale.
#
# Rebuilt by `make analyse` in about 35 seconds. 22 files, ~2.7 MB.

# --- Editor scratch -------------------------------------------------------
# Vim writes a .swp file beside whatever you are editing. They are session
# state, they are meaningless outside the editor that made them, and they
# appear mid-commit when someone saves and commits in a hurry.
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# --- Scratch --------------------------------------------------------------
.mplcache/
_private/
